   "text": "Reset view"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "id": "solid-intro",
   "text": "Keep the bottom copy of Z. Collapse the whole top copy to one point."
  },
  {
   "tag": "h2",
   "id": "solid-left-title",
   "text": "Z × [0,1] · cylinder"
  },
  {
   "tag": "h2",
   "id": "solid-right-title",
   "text": "Collapse the top copy of Z"
  },
  {
   "tag": "button",
   "id": "solid-play",
   "text": "▶ Play"
  },
  {
   "tag": "label",
   "id": null,
   "text": "Collapse λ0.00"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "id": "solid-hint",
   "text": "Drag either shape to rotate both · arrow keys also rotate."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "id": "solid-equation",
   "text": "Cone(Z) = (Z × [0,1]) / (Z × {1})"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "id": "solid-status",
   "text": "The top is 100% of its original width. The identification happens at 1. The cone on an edge is a filled triangle."
  },
  {
   "tag": "summary",
   "id": null,
   "text": "What am I looking at?"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "id": null,
   "text": "A cylinder keeps a separate copy of Z at every height. The cone identifies every point at height 1 to the same apex. This is a quotient construction."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "id": null,
   "text": "For a circle base, there are no filled caps: the cylinder and cone here are surfaces. Choosing a filled disk includes every interior point and produces a solid cylinder or solid cone."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "id": null,
   "text": "The intermediate pictures show how the top approaches the identification. Here Cone(Z) names a space; C(A) in the other views names an assigned carrier region."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "id": null,
   "text": "Carrier proof · pp. 21–22 · Cones · Hatcher, Chapter 0"
  }
 ],
